WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.480 --> 00:00:05.610
I don’t know why but I always enjoyed listening
to christmas Carols. Maybe it’s due to their

00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:09.559
connection with my favourite season or maybe
they’re just good songs, i don’t know.

00:00:09.559 --> 00:00:13.520
But with that said if I had to pick my favorite,
I will probably have to go with “Good King

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:17.670
Wenceslas” , after all it’s very well
known even being sung by the soldiers in the

00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:21.840
christmas truce. But to specify, when i said
“Good King Wenceslas” I meant that I like

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:24.970
the translated czech version not the english
one …

00:00:24.970 --> 00:00:32.189
But wait hold on, translated in to Czech?
Isn’t Wenceslas a Czech king, … hhmm yehhh

00:00:32.189 --> 00:00:38.260
he is, well not this one, or this, or this
one, not any of them, in fact the king we

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:42.539
call the Good King Wenceslas or also known
as St. Wenceslas the patron saint of Czech

00:00:42.539 --> 00:00:46.960
Lands lived in the 10th century and was the
first king of Bohemia ever to be called Wenceslas,

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:48.600
So all these other guys are just posers.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:52.560
But digress, now that we know which Wenceslau
of bohemia the song is talking about, why

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:57.829
isn’t the original carol in Czech? Well
for that we will have to do some extensive

00:00:57.829 --> 00:01:02.589
digging as the answer isn’t as simple as
you may think. So to start off we will have

00:01:02.589 --> 00:01:07.720
to skip St Wenceslaus actual life in the 10th
century as the origin of the carol surprisingly

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:11.950
doesn’t start with him. It was in fact during
the high middle ages around the early 13th

00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:16.140
century when the melody and the trochaic meter
of the song was created, but it wasn’t a

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:20.700
christmas carol. It was a latin spring song
called "Tempus adest floridum" which talked

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:25.460
about blooming flowers and the coming of warmth
right after the frost of winter. This song

00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:29.240
wasn’t written down for much of its early
existence so no one really knows where it

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:41.680
came from. But this is how it sounded like
…

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:46.280
So pretty much very similar to the Wenceslau
Carol today just with different words.

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:50.470
Continuing on from there Tempus adest Floridum
was finally written down in Finland around

00:01:50.470 --> 00:01:55.680
the 16th century in to a book called Piae
Cantiones. This book which was a collection

00:01:55.680 --> 00:02:00.010
of multiple medieval songs eventually found
its way to England where in the 19th century

00:02:00.010 --> 00:02:02.860
it came in to the hands of John Mason Neale.

00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:08.229
He was known in England as a devout christian,
hymn writer and specially a hymn translator

00:02:08.229 --> 00:02:13.079
of old latin medieval texts. And this is where
Tempus adest floridum comes in to play, as

00:02:13.079 --> 00:02:17.920
he is the one who took the melody of that
song and used it for a christmas song he wrote

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:22.680
called “Good King Wenceslas”. He then
published this song in 1853 which was meat

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:27.439
with mixed results, however over time it became
one of the most well known christmas carols

00:02:27.439 --> 00:02:31.170
not just in the English speaking world but
also in the rest of Europe.

00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:36.569
So when does Czech come in to play to any
of this? Well if you look closely on the wikipedia

00:02:36.569 --> 00:02:40.989
article for the Good King Wenceslas carol,
there’s a sentence mentioning that, According

00:02:40.989 --> 00:02:46.700
to older Czech sources, Neale's lyrics are
a translation of a poem by Czech poet Václav

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:51.680
Alois Svoboda, written in Czech, German and
Latin. Why those three languages you ask,

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:56.209
well because Bohemia was part of the Austro-Hungarian
empire, and if you wrote something in czech

00:02:56.209 --> 00:03:00.219
you also had to write it at least in German
and Latin so other people in the Empire could

00:03:00.219 --> 00:03:05.129
read it. But I digress, again. Continuing
from there in true wikipedia fashion when

00:03:05.129 --> 00:03:08.520
you click the source for this sentence the
page doesn’t exist.

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:12.890
Does this mean that the Czechs just made this
up so they could lay some sort of a hypothetical

00:03:12.890 --> 00:03:16.980
claim to a popular english christmas carol
about their old king. Well that’s what I

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:21.909
thought after googling for the original poem
written by Vaclav and only coming across numerous

00:03:21.909 --> 00:03:26.969
pages like this one on duolingo where even
people from Czech republic where unable to

00:03:26.969 --> 00:03:31.579
find the original poem. With my luck being
no different, I was almost about to decide

00:03:31.579 --> 00:03:35.540
that the poem was just made up Czech Propaganda
and leave it at that.

00:03:35.540 --> 00:03:41.811
But then I came across this book written in
Czech in 1902 where on the page 208 the writer

00:03:41.811 --> 00:03:46.430
complains about Vaclav not getting any credit
for writing the original “Good King Wenceslas”

00:03:46.430 --> 00:03:52.031
poem in 1847 which would be 6 years before
Neals publication of his carol. The writer

00:03:52.031 --> 00:03:57.379
goes on to sight his source where he names
the poem in german Sanct Wenzel und Podiwini.

00:03:57.379 --> 00:04:02.200
Which means Saint Wenceslas and Podiwin ? Whatever
that is.

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:07.219
But now I have a source, so I can just plug
this in to google and find the original. Well

00:04:07.219 --> 00:04:13.099
turns out no. Because even mighty google has
no idea what this source means. Even if you

00:04:13.099 --> 00:04:17.400
just put in the supposed german name of the
poem, nothing turns out. So now I am back

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:22.160
to square one as the Austrian, Czech, German
and Oxford online library archives along with

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:26.190
google turn out nothing when you type in the
supposed name of the poem outside of that

00:04:26.190 --> 00:04:28.670
one book that is referencing it.

00:04:28.670 --> 00:04:32.760
But at this point I am not willing to give
up just yet. I decided to change my approach

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:37.560
from searching just for the poem to searching
for the author of the poem. Although at that

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:42.420
moment I realized I completely forgot to address
the problem every Austro-Hungarian scholar

00:04:42.420 --> 00:04:48.090
faces and that is multiple names and variations
of names for one thing. So when it came to

00:04:48.090 --> 00:04:53.940
the author of our supposed poem, turns out
he had 15+ different variations of his name.

00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:58.350
These variations weren’t large but when
dealing with library databases a single letter

00:04:58.350 --> 00:05:03.410
change can make a big difference. Plus, the
authors first name Vaclav is the Czech name

00:05:03.410 --> 00:05:08.031
from which the english Wenceslas is derived
from. So I am searching for a poem with king

00:05:08.031 --> 00:05:17.200
named Wenceslas and the writer named Wenceslas.
Or Wenceslas and Vaclav, or Vaclac and Vaclav?

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:24.650
Nein, er heißt Wenzel. Nie ma na imię Wacław.
Nem a neve Wenceshlaus. (slures) Pred oknom,

00:05:24.650 --> 00:05:32.890
za oknom, stoji mikulas, povedz nam dedusko
… ha co tu vsetci robyte ? Aghhhhh, lets

00:05:32.890 --> 00:05:34.590
go back to the drawing board.

00:05:34.590 --> 00:05:39.370
Now we know there’s multiple variations
for not just the name of the author but also

00:05:39.370 --> 00:05:44.250
the poem. So we could have just been searching
with a bad variation of the poem's name. I

00:05:44.250 --> 00:05:49.290
quickly write out some plausible name variations
and go back to the aforementioned online databases.

00:05:49.290 --> 00:05:54.390
I still have no luck finding the exact poem
but I do find numerous amounts of writings

00:05:54.390 --> 00:06:00.370
mentioning or referencing the 1847 Wenceslas
poem. I also find that Podivin isn’t a weird

00:06:00.370 --> 00:06:05.220
german word but an actual name of the king's
favorite servant in the 10th century. I even

00:06:05.220 --> 00:06:09.410
manage to find the book keepings of either
a Prague based publisher or just a bookseller

00:06:09.410 --> 00:06:14.520
(I can’t tell) selling the Sanct Wenzeslaw
und Podiwin poem for 10 Austro-Hungarian crowns

00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:20.080
in 1847. Now finally convinced the actual
poem exists, I am poised to finally find it.

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:24.750
So at this point it’s time to turn to my
last plan which is searching for all the different

00:06:24.750 --> 00:06:29.371
variations of the authors name and then looking
if one of the works that come up is the Good

00:06:29.371 --> 00:06:34.520
King Wenceslas poem. I go to the Austrian
National library first, and nothing. I then

00:06:34.520 --> 00:06:38.870
go to the Czech national library and after
a few tries of different name variations I

00:06:38.870 --> 00:06:46.330
finally find the Czech and German version
of the poem. But, i can’t see them, because

00:06:46.330 --> 00:06:51.690
they’re not digitised. I can however schedule
a meeting and fly to Prague to see the works

00:06:51.690 --> 00:06:54.560
with my own eyes … apparently.

00:06:54.560 --> 00:06:58.441
This is so weird. You complain for a Czech
not getting credit for creating the original

00:06:58.441 --> 00:07:03.310
Good King Wenceslas poem but then hide the
actual original poems from anyone reading

00:07:03.310 --> 00:07:08.140
it. You don’t have to take pictures of the
originals if it will ruin them, at least write

00:07:08.140 --> 00:07:13.560
them down on a blog post or something so we
can at least see what the original poem says

00:07:13.560 --> 00:07:21.360
… Unless, it doesn’t actually exist. Yes
there are many references citing the poem,

00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:26.130
but we never seen the original. Maybe this
is just a weird cycle where everybody is references

00:07:26.130 --> 00:07:30.311
something that doesn’t actually exist. And
it got to such a point that the Czech National

00:07:30.311 --> 00:07:35.200
library mentions the poems under the writers
creations without really looking if they actually

00:07:35.200 --> 00:07:39.790
have them. Brushing it off as in ou it’s
in just an old pile of books somewhere maybe.

00:07:39.790 --> 00:07:45.340
But they don’t actually have it. That would
be crazy, that would never happen, right?

00:07:45.340 --> 00:07:52.470
Well, actually, in this case it didn’t happen.
How do I know. Because even though I haven't

00:07:52.470 --> 00:07:58.060
seen the original poem a week later I managed
to find an 1847 Austrian Yearbook for teachers,

00:07:58.060 --> 00:08:02.610
parents and educators which along with math
problems and other things that teachers would

00:08:02.610 --> 00:08:08.539
like has the German version of Vaclav Alois
Svoboda’s Sanct Wenceſlaw und Podiwin poem,

00:08:08.539 --> 00:08:09.920
published that same year.

00:08:09.920 --> 00:08:15.180
Now finally that we have the poem we can look
if Neal actually just translated Vaclav’s

00:08:15.180 --> 00:08:16.970
poem or created his own song. Disclaimer keep
in mind my German isn’t great but I’ll

00:08:16.970 --> 00:08:17.970
try my best.

00:08:17.970 --> 00:08:20.930
What we can say for sure just from the first
look is that Neal didn’t just straight up

00:08:20.930 --> 00:08:26.060
translate the poem into english and that was
that, this is because Neal’s carol has only

00:08:26.060 --> 00:08:32.680
5 stanzas where as Vaclav’s poem has 3 verses
together numbering in 26 stanzas. Plus Neal’s

00:08:32.680 --> 00:08:36.740
carol has an alternate rhyming scheme (ABABCDCD...)
as opposed to Vaclav’s poem which has 2

00:08:36.740 --> 00:08:40.319
different rhyming schemes starting with a
couplet (AABBCC…) which then switches in

00:08:40.319 --> 00:08:45.220
to an alternate. This means without alterations
Vaclav’s poem couldn’t even be sung to

00:08:45.220 --> 00:08:47.250
the good King Wenceslas tune.

00:08:47.250 --> 00:08:51.980
However when you start looking at the context
of the poem, that’s where things get interesting.

00:08:51.980 --> 00:08:56.190
Both works start of with a clear moonlit night
with snow on the ground. Both have the King

00:08:56.190 --> 00:09:00.830
Looking out of his Window, Both have the King
Spotting some poor person/people struggling

00:09:00.830 --> 00:09:05.779
in the winter night, both have the king deciding
he needs to help the said poor person/people,

00:09:05.779 --> 00:09:10.421
both a servant helping the king carry wine,
food and wood, to the poor person/people both

00:09:10.421 --> 00:09:14.870
have the servant struggling to move any longer
in the cold and both have the king telling

00:09:14.870 --> 00:09:18.870
the servant to walk in his footsteps as it
will be easier for him.

00:09:18.870 --> 00:09:24.850
In conclusion it seems too coincidental for
there to be so many similarities between these

00:09:24.850 --> 00:09:30.279
two works. Therefore it’s safe to say that
Neal definitely took the story of Good King

00:09:30.279 --> 00:09:35.639
Wenceslas from Vaclav’s poem. After that,
he shortened it, altered it to fit the tune

00:09:35.639 --> 00:09:39.959
and published it, without crediting or even
mentioning Vaclav at all.

00:09:39.959 --> 00:09:44.690
So next time when you hear the Good King Wenceslas
carol you’ll know it’s a story about a

00:09:44.690 --> 00:09:50.240
10th century Bohemian king from a 19th century
czech poet altered by an English hymn writer

00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:53.910
to fit a 13th century spring tune.

00:09:53.910 --> 00:09:57.560
